# The carlos-emr/carlos-ctl release whose .deb every carlos release
# re-attaches (deb-packages.yml, via debian/fetch-carlos-ctl.sh) so one
# release page carries the whole install, and which the install tests
# (scripts/deb-upgrade-verify.sh) exercise. The tag IS the Debian version
# (carlos-ctl is a native package): the asset is carlos-ctl_<tag>_all.deb,
# with '-' read as '.' in a pre-release asset name and '~' in dpkg.
#
# This pin and debian/control's `carlos-ctl (>= ...)` floor are different
# things: the floor is the OLDEST CLI this package's maintainer scripts can
# run with, the pin is the CLI a release SHIPS. Keep the pin at or above the
# floor. Raise the floor when the postinst starts using a new verb or flag;
# raise the pin when a CLI release should reach operators with the next
# carlos release.
tag=1.1.2
#
# 1.1.2 carries two carlos-ctl fixes this package relies on: import-o19
# holds the database-ownership lock for the whole import (#3678, which the
# Depends floor in debian/control requires), and the bulk copy keeps
# UNIQUE_CHECKS on, so a duplicate unique key stops the copy with ERROR 1062
# (value redacted) before the table is checkpointed, instead of MariaDB
# storing no rows and reporting success (#4100).
#
# Until that tag is PUBLISHED, CI (debian-python-tests.yml,
# script-regressions.yml) checks out this commit of carlos-emr/carlos-ctl
# instead, so the contract tests run against the CLI this release will pin
# before the CLI release exists. It is carlos-ctl main after both fixes
# merged. The release build never uses it
# (debian/fetch-carlos-ctl.sh downloads the tag's published asset or
# fails). Drop the line, or leave it: once the tag exists it is not read.
fallback=dec20b977218639d5c8484e892f105b91935cd74
